Or if greedy, accept if target argmax matches draft. For stochastic sampling, rejection sampling preserves exact target distribution. Because target model is authoritative and correction step fixes mismatches.\n\nSpeedup: target forward pass can verify multiple tokens at once due to parallelism; if many accepted, get k+1 tokens per target pass instead of one. Overhead: draft model cost, target verification cost, memory/batching, acceptance rate. In practice speedup determined by acceptance rate, draft length, relative cost of draft vs target, hardware parallelism, batch size, sequence length, memory bandwidth. End with two most common reasons speedup smaller than hoped: low acceptance rate (draft model mismatch, hard/long-tail tasks, high temperature) and overhead/parallelism limits (draft cost, target verification not free, batch size/memory, latency, small k, hardware not enough parallelism). Need end with two most common reasons. Could be: (1) draft tokens are often rejected, especially on difficult or diverse prompts; (2) verification and draft generation add overhead and hardware parallelism/batching limits reduce gains.\n\nNeed word count. Let's count. Draft:\n\n\"Speculative decoding speeds up large language model inference by using a small draft model to propose several future tokens before the large target model is run. The draft model generates k candidate tokens sequentially, which is cheap because it is smaller and can be run quickly.", "top2": [[[1596, -0.001898301183246076], [22916, -6.763105869293213]], [[1144, -1.2040211004205048e-05], [513, -12.235730171203613]], [[4087, -0.20738521218299866], [310, -1.6886054277420044]], [[1156, -0.00215368764474988], [303, -6.217634677886963]], [[579, -0.21644055843353271], [25, -1.8507179021835327]], [[1622, -0.009743379428982735], [3296, -4.832197666168213]], [[25, -0.29710814356803894], [13, -1.3769975900650024]], [[59987, -0.13858944177627563], [7860, -2.4076895713806152]], [[303, -0.27865588665008545], [3835,